              {
                "name": "exec_start",
                "title": "Start Session",
                "description": "Start a process as a session; returns session_id at once. Use when it outlives the call, streams output or reads stdin (exec_write). Then exec_poll, exec_wait, exec_kill, exec_close. Sessions survive link drops, hub restarts and client disconnects (they live in the node process, not the connection); after a reconnect, poll the same session_id from your last offsets. Finished sessions are reaped after the session TTL. Durability is opt in: durable:true persists the session under the node state dir so it survives a node restart; after the restart poll the same session_id and a recovered session answers recovered:true with stdin_available:false. Durable needs MCP_NODE_STATE_DIR or --state-dir (else DurabilityDisabled); the state dir is locked for the node lifetime (a second node answers StateDirLocked at startup); cwd plus argv must fit the 12 KiB record cap with at most 256 argv elements (DurableMetadataTooLarge); durable sessions are Linux only in v0.5.0 (DurabilityUnsupported). Without durable a node restart drops the session.",
                "inputSchema": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "argv": {
                      "type": "array",
                      "items": {
                        "type": "string"
                      },
                      "description": "Program and arguments, e.g. [\"python3\", \"-i\"]. Passed verbatim. With durable:true: at most 256 elements, and cwd+argv must fit the 12 KiB record cap (DurableMetadataTooLarge)."
                    },
                    "cwd": {
                      "type": "string",
                      "description": "Child working directory; omit to inherit."
                    },
                    "durable": {
                      "type": "boolean",
                      "description": "Persist this session to the state dir so it survives a node restart; requires MCP_NODE_STATE_DIR/--state-dir, else DurabilityDisabled"
                    }
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "argv"
                  ]
                },
                "outputSchema": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "ok": {
                      "type": "boolean"
                    },
                    "session_id": {
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    "pid": {
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    "recovered": {
                      "type": "boolean"
                    },
                    "stdin_available": {
                      "type": "boolean"
                    },
                    "error": {
                      "type": "string"
                    }
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "ok"
                  ]
                },
                "annotations": {
                  "readOnlyHint": false,
                  "destructiveHint": true,
                  "idempotentHint": false,
                  "openWorldHint": true
                }
              },
              {
                "name": "exec_poll",
                "title": "Poll Session",
                "description": "Read new session output from byte offsets: stdout/stderr deltas, done, exit_code, truncation flags. Pass back the offsets from the previous reply to resume. Works across reconnects: offsets are positions in the node-side buffer, which survives link drops and client disconnects. Unknown/expired session: {ok:false, error:unknown_session, session_id, expired}; expired=true means this node recently had it and closed/reaped/evicted it (bounded recency window; ids removed long ago answer expired=false). done reports process exit, not drained output: keep polling while has_more is true (done and has_more can both be true on the exit page). Same payload as exec_wait. Durable sessions add recovered, stdin_available, output_complete and tree_state, and a finished one adds completion_reason (interrupted, or orphaned_exit when the child outlived the node). A recovered session has no stdin left from the dead node: stdin_available is false and exec_write answers StdinUnavailable. On POSIX a child that exited while the node was down is reaped by init, so its exit code is unknowable: exit_code is null with completion_reason orphaned_exit while output_complete stays true because the logs are exact.",
                "inputSchema": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "session_id": {
                      "type": "integer",
                      "description": "Positive id from exec_start."
                    },
                    "stdout_offset": {
                      "type": "integer",
                      "description": "Stdout byte offset to resume from, as returned by the previous poll (default 0)."
                    },
                    "stderr_offset": {
                      "type": "integer",
                      "description": "Stderr byte offset to resume from, as returned by the previous poll (default 0)."
                    },
                    "max_bytes": {
                      "type": "integer",
                      "minimum": 4,
                      "maximum": 1048576,
                      "description": "Per-stream raw byte page size; also bounded by MCP_NODE_MAX_OUT. Offsets advance only by consumed bytes."
                    }
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "session_id"
                  ]
                },
